@preamble use("list")
map
Applies a function to each element of a list and returns a new list
containing the results.

map(list, f)

map([1, 2, 3], (x) { x * 2 })    # [2, 4, 6]
===
filter
Returns a new list containing only elements for which the predicate
function returns true.

filter(list, f)

filter([1, 2, 3, 4], (x) { x > 2 })    # [3, 4]
===
reduce
Reduces a list to a single value by applying a function that takes
an accumulator and each element.

reduce(list, initial, f)

reduce([1, 2, 3], 0, (acc, x) { acc + x })    # 6
===
each
@unchecked
Iterates over each element calling the function for side effects.
Returns the original list.

each(list, f)

each([1, 2, 3], (x) { println(x) })
===
sum
Returns the sum of all elements in the list.

sum(list)

sum([1, 2, 3, 4])    # 10
===
max
Returns the maximum element in the list.
Returns [@error, :empty, "empty list"] for empty list.

max(list)

max([3, 1, 4, 1, 5])    # 5
===
min
Returns the minimum element in the list.
Returns [@error, :empty, "empty list"] for empty list.

min(list)

min([3, 1, 4, 1, 5])    # 1
===
reverse
Returns a new list with elements in reverse order.

reverse(list)

reverse([1, 2, 3])    # [3, 2, 1]
===
find
Returns the first element for which the predicate returns true.
Returns [@error, :lookup, "not found"] if no element matches.

find(list, f)

find([1, 2, 3, 4], (x) { x > 2 })    # 3
===
any
Returns true if the predicate returns true for any element.

any(list, f)

any([1, 2, 3], (x) { x > 2 })    # true
===
all
Returns true if the predicate returns true for all elements.

all(list, f)

all([2, 4, 6], (x) { x > 0 })    # true
===
concat
Returns a new list with all elements of a followed by all elements of b.

concat(a, b)

concat([1, 2], [3, 4])    # [1, 2, 3, 4]
===
range
Returns a list of integers from start (inclusive) to end (exclusive).

range(start, end)

range(0, 5)    # [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]
range(3, 7)    # [3, 4, 5, 6]
===
sort
Returns a stable sorted copy of list, leaving the input unchanged. With no
comparator, sorting uses Aiki natural scalar ordering: numbers numerically,
strings by rune sequence, runes by code point, and symbols by name. Other values
require a comparator. comparator(a, b) must return a boolean that is true when a
comes before b.

sort(list)
sort(list, comparator)

sort([3, 1, 2])    # [1, 2, 3]
sort(["beta", "alpha"])    # ["alpha", "beta"]
sort([3, 1, 2], (a, b) { a > b })    # [3, 2, 1]
sort(["bbb", "a", "cc"], (a, b) { length(a) < length(b) })    # ["a", "cc", "bbb"]
===
